>h2 Overview
>tree open Overview
CupidOS supports hardware x87 and SSE/SSE2 floating point in the kernel,
language, assembler, libm, and printf.

  - Build: `-mfpmath=sse -msse -msse2 -mstackrealign` in CFLAGS.
  - Init: `fpu_init()` is the first call in `kmain`; every AP calls the
    no-log `fpu_init_cpu()` before any other C operation or compiler-generated
    SSE. That helper is built with target("general-regs-only") so its own
    setup cannot use floating registers. CR0 and CR4 are per-CPU: each path
    sets
    CR0.EM=0/MP=1/NE=1/TS=0 and
    CR4.OSFXSR=1/OSXMMEXCPT=1, runs FNINIT, and loads MXCSR=0x1F80.
  - Context switch: `context_switch.asm` FXSAVEs the outgoing PCB's
    `fp_state[512]` and FXRSTORs the incoming task's. Eager: no CR0.TS games.
  - PCB: `process_t.fp_state` is 512 bytes at 16-byte alignment. Offset 80.
  - Exceptions: IDT vectors 7 (#NM), 16 (#MF), 19 (#XF) route to
    `fpu_nm_handler`/`fpu_mf_handler`/`fpu_xf_handler`, which call `panic_fpu`
    with a serial dump of FSW/FCW/MXCSR. Should never fire under masked
    MXCSR + eager switch.
>endtree

>h2 CupidC FP Types
>tree open CupidC FP Types
  float:   4 bytes, SSE scalar.
  double:  8 bytes, SSE scalar.
  float4:  16 bytes, SSE packed (4 floats).
  double2: 16 bytes, SSE packed (2 doubles).

>h3 Shared Self-Hosting Path
The shared compiler used for self-hosting combines its i386 x87 transport and
arithmetic path with SSE conversion and comparison emission. It carries
non-atomic float and double values through objects, calls, variadic reads, and
returns. Explicit casts and assignment conversion work in either direction.
Matching floating conditional arms keep their width. Mixed arithmetic and
conditional arms use double.

The +=, -=, *=, and /= operators compute at the common width, convert the
stored result back to the left type, and evaluate the left designator once.
Each changed x87 result is stored at its C width before the next Linear IR
instruction.

Decimal constants, represented integer conversions, mixed integer-and-floating
arithmetic, and all six comparisons use the shared SSE path. A mixed floating
comparison uses double, and only != is true for an unordered NaN input.

The checked i386 Linux seed now carries static floating constant data and this
complete comparison path. The normal build uses them in kernel/gfx/jpeg.cc
and kernel/gfx/glyph_raster.cc. Their deterministic checked objects and guest
JPEG and glyph paths pass. ADR 0139 records the production transfer.

The checked seed understands the general-regs-only target on canonical
file-scope functions. It rejects compiler-generated floating instructions,
values, and call arguments while explicit source assembly keeps its separate
contract. It also accepts the exact ldmxcsr %0 memory input and
emits 0F AE 10 through the shared x86 model. The complete fpu_init_cpu body
passes unchanged. It also accepts the exact MOVSS float-memory round trip in
fpu_boot_smoke and its one-way load and store forms. Each form requires the
xmm0 clobber and emits F3 0F 10 00 or F3 0F 11 00 through EAX. The unchanged
stress_sin x87 block is also represented. It keeps one double output and one
double input, permits no clobbers, and emits FLD, FSIN, and FSTP through the
shared x86 model. The x87 stack returns to its original depth and no frame
temporary is used. The earlier compiler proof used kernel/cpu/fpu.c. The
unchanged implementation is now kernel/cpu/fpu.cc, a checked CupidC production
root. Two checked compiles produce the same validated 6,620-byte object with
SHA-256
14c3ea232b7d4455ceabd561c69293cc5849abae24d9f210aa69d64ed8c8a5cb.
Four-vCPU e1000 and RTL8139 boots print both FPU boot markers and finish
feature16_asm_fpu.cc successfully.

The checked seed represents the exact x87 round-down statement in
str_floor. It takes one double memory output and one double memory input with
the exact ax and memory clobbers. Emission saves the incoming x87 control word
below ESP, selects round toward negative infinity, executes FRNDINT, restores
the saved word, and stores the result. It reuses the consumed input-address
slot for scratch, leaves the pending output address intact, and needs no frame
temporary.

A bounded shared-decoder oracle checks eight binary64 inputs under all four
incoming rounding modes. It verifies exact results, balanced x87 depth, and
control-word restoration without executing native x87 code. Two exact
compiles of the unchanged helper produce the same 420-byte object. Full
unchanged kernel/core/string.c still stops at its separate
double-to-uint64_t cast on line 190, so production ownership does not move.

Compiler head also represents the x87 power statements in libm_pow_impl and
libm_powf_impl. The double form has five double memory operands. The mixed
form has a float output, two float inputs, and two double inputs. Both require
one memory clobber. Linear IR evaluates each set of five addresses once in
source order. Each 116-byte focused function contains seventeen x87
instructions, uses DC E1 for FSUBR ST(1), ST(0), reaches stack depth three,
and returns to its incoming depth without a relocation.

Compiler head also represents the exact volatile sqrtsd %1, %0 statement in
libm_sqrt_impl. It accepts one modifiable, non-atomic double =x output, one
non-atomic double x input, and no clobbers. Linear IR evaluates the output
address before the input value. The 65-byte focused function uses the shared
MOVSD and SQRTSD encodings and has no relocations.

Compiler head also represents the exact volatile x87 statement in
libm_atan2_impl. It accepts one modifiable, non-atomic double =m output, two
addressable, non-atomic double m inputs in y, x order, and one memory
clobber. Linear IR evaluates all three addresses once in source order. The
53-byte focused function uses the shared model for both loads, FPATAN, and
the final store, with no relocations.

Compiler head also represents the exact volatile x87 statement in
libm_exp_impl. It accepts one modifiable, non-atomic double =m output, two
addressable, non-atomic double m inputs in x, log2e order, and one memory
clobber. Linear IR evaluates all three addresses once in source order. The
71-byte focused function has no relocations, reaches x87 depth three, and
returns to its incoming depth.

Compiler head also represents the exact aligned fabs mask block and the
following fabs and fabsf wrappers. The masks occupy the first 32 bytes of
.rodata, with local labels at offsets 0 and 16. The double wrapper uses MOVSD
and ANDPD in 15 text bytes. The float wrapper uses MOVSS and ANDPS in 14
bytes. Each has one absolute relocation to its mask. The unchanged source now
stops at the floor wrapper on line 281. The checked seed does not yet carry
these compiler-head capabilities, so
kernel/cpu/libm.c remains host-owned.

Direct floating truth, a floating controlling expression, increment and
decrement, hexadecimal or subnormal constants, long double, SIMD, and atomic
floating access remain outside this shared path. The SSE details below
describe the private in-OS compiler.

>h3 Arithmetic
Binary operators `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` work on scalar float/double via SSE
scalar opcodes (ADDSS/ADDSD/etc.). Implicit promotion:
  `int + float -> float`, `float + double -> double`.
SIMD types need matching types for `+/-/*//`; mixing scalar and SIMD is a
compile error.

>h3 Casts
`(int)3.7` -> 3 (truncating). `(float)5` -> 5.0. `(double)1.5f` widens.
Casts lower to CVTSI2SS/CVTTSS2SI/CVTSS2SD/etc.

>h3 Element Access
`v.x / v.y / v.z / v.w` on float4 extracts a scalar float via SHUFPS.
`v.x / v.y` on double2 via SHUFPD.

>h3 Intrinsics
`_mm_*_ps` (17 variants) and `_mm_*_pd` (11 variants) are recognized by name
and inlined as SSE opcodes. See `kernel/cpu/simd_intrin.h` for the full list.
>endtree

>h2 libm
>tree open libm
25 operations (50 symbols with f-variants).

Hardware fast-paths:
  sqrt, sin, cos, tan, atan, atan2, fabs, floor, ceil, round,
  trunc, fmod, exp2, log2.

Composite:
  exp, log, pow, asin, acos, sinh, cosh, tanh, cbrt, hypot, nextafter.

>box
libm functions use a CupidC-internal ABI and return results in XMM0 instead of
ST(0), as System V expects. Default GCC code in the kernel cannot call these
functions directly. CupidC JIT code reaches them through the BIND_T table.
>endbox
>endtree

>h2 CupidASM Opcodes
>tree open CupidASM Opcodes
CupidASM supports about 80 FPU and SSE opcodes in these groups:

  FPU state control (FXSAVE, FXRSTOR, FINIT, FNINIT, FWAIT, LDMXCSR, STMXCSR)
  SSE scalar  (23): MOVSS/SD, ADDSS/SD, and more.
  SSE packed  (24): MOVAPS/UPS, ADDPS/PD, SHUFPS, CMPPS, and more.
  x87         (25): FLD, FSIN, FPATAN, F2XM1, FYL2X, and more.

Register tokens: XMM0-7 and ST0-7.
>endtree

>h2 Testing
>tree open Testing
Test programs:
  `bin/feature12_float.cc`    scalar float arithmetic, casts, element access.
  `bin/feature13_double.cc`   double + transcendentals.
  `bin/feature14_simd.cc`     float4/double2 + intrinsics.
  `bin/feature15_libm.cc`     cycle 8 functions x 7 inputs vs glibc reference.
  `bin/feature16_asm_fpu.cc`  CupidC inline asm using SSE + x87.
  `bin/fp_drill.cc`           manual #XF provocation (panics kernel).
  `demos/fpu_kernel.asm`      CupidASM FPU state + scalar + packed + x87.
  `demos/simd_blur.asm`       SIMD box blur via MOVUPS/ADDPS/MULPS.

Interactive: boot QEMU graphical, type the command in the shell.

[Run float feature test](shell:cc /bin/feature12_float.cc)
>endtree

>h2 Stress Test
>tree open Stress Test
fpu_context_stress() is defined in kernel/cpu/fpu.cc, but the normal boot does
not call it. The checked CupidC production recipe does not consume host
CFLAGS, so the old -DFPU_STRESS instruction is no longer a valid activation
path.
Panics on corruption.
>endtree

>h2 Exception Drill
>tree open Exception Drill
`fp_drill` shell command unmasks DE in MXCSR and divides by zero, verifying
the #XF handler panics with MXCSR dump.

>box
Running `fp_drill` requires a kernel reboot afterward.
>endbox
>endtree

>h2 Verification
>box
How to verify P1 FPU/SSE:

cc /bin/feature12_float.cc

Expected output: PASS

[Run now](shell:cc /bin/feature12_float.cc)
>endbox

